[12.380622, "o", "\u001b[5;3HTotals from /tmp/caf-pr1603-accepted/orders/report.json:\u001b[6;86H\u001b[1K\r\n  currency  count  total\r\n  ──────────────────────\u001b[56X\r\n  EUR       2      5.30\u001b[57X\r\n  USD       3      24.75\u001b[60X\u001b[11;86H\u001b[1K\r\n  Files: /tmp/caf-pr1603-accepted/orders/summarize.py,\r\n  /tmp/caf-pr1603-accepted/orders/report.json. Run with `python3 summarize.py\r\n  [orders.csv] [report.json]`.\u001b[16;1H  Assumptions I had to make:\u001b[58X\u001b[17;86H\u001b[1K\r\n  · Refunded is counted. docs/requirements.md says only \"Ignore cancelled orders,\" so\u001b[19;5HA6 USD 5.00 `refunded` is included in USD. If refunded should also be excluded,\u001b[20;5HUSD becomes count=2 total=19.75. A6 is the only non-paid, non-cancelled row, so\u001b[21;5Hthis is the one judgement call affecting the numbers.\u001b[24X\r\n  · Totals are JSON strings (\"24.75\", \"5.30\"), counts are integers — so the decimal\u001b[23;5Hvalue survives serialization exactly instead of becoming a float like `24.75` vs\u001b[24;5H`24.750000000000004`.\r\n  · A3 USD 100.00 cancelled is excluded, per the requirement.\r\n  · trace_export.py writes its own `export-receipt.json`; it is separate from this\u001b[27;5Hrequest and I did not run it.\u001b[29;1H  To reproduce: EUR = 3.10 + 2.20 = 5.30; USD = 12.50 + 7.25 + 5.00 = 24.75.\u001b[31;32H· ◇ YOLO ───    $0.02 · ⟲ 81% cached   24.6k/1M · 2%   checking ·\u001b[32;4H"]
